  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that ${5}^{x+4}={2}^{2-x},$ what is the value of $x?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we take ${log}_{5}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x+4} & ={2}^{2-x} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{x+4}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({2}^{2-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+4={log}_{5}⁡({2}^{2-x})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+4=(2-x){log}_{5}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =(2-x){log}_{5}⁡(2) \\\\ x+4 & =2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-x{log}_{5}⁡(2) \\\\ x+x{log}_{5}⁡(2) & =2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-4 \\\\ x(1+{log}_{5}⁡(2)) & =2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-4}{1+{log}_{5}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡(2)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}≈0.430\\,677.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2\\cdot 0.430\\,677-4}{1+0.430\\,677}≈-2.194,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
